course: physics
lesson: 16
title: Pressure tricks: pumps, tyres and vacuums
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Air always moves from where it is squashed and presses harder to where it presses less, and many machines in your home are clever ways of using that one rule.
explain: A vacuum cleaner does not really suck: its fan throws air out of the back, the pressure inside drops, and the outside air rushes in at the nozzle, carrying the crumbs with it.
explain: When you breathe in, a muscle under your lungs pulls down and makes more room, the pressure in your lungs drops, and the air outside pushes in, just like water into a syringe when you pull the plunger.
explain: Warmth changes pressure too: a warm gas pushes harder, a cold one pushes less, so a balloon in the freezer shrinks and a jar sealed hot pulls its lid in as it cools.
explain: Water also cares about pressure: under a heavy lid it can get hotter than 100 degrees before boiling, and on a high mountain it boils before it gets that hot.
fact: A vacuum cleaner's fan pushes air out, which lowers the pressure inside, so the outside air pushes in at the nozzle carrying dust.
fact: A punctured tyre goes flat because the squashed air inside escapes through the hole to the lower pressure outside.
fact: In soapy water the air escaping from a punctured tube makes bubbles that show where the hole is.
fact: Squeezing a ketchup bottle raises the pressure inside, and a push on a closed bottle spreads to every part, so the sauce is pushed out of the hole; when you let go, the bottle springs back and outside air is pushed in with a gurgle.
fact: Pulling back a syringe's plunger makes more room inside, the pressure drops, and the outside air pushes water in.
fact: When you breathe in, a muscle under your lungs pulls down, your chest gets bigger, the pressure inside drops, and outside air pushes in.
fact: A fizzy drink has gas squashed into it under high pressure, and when you open it the pressure drops and the gas escapes as bubbles with a hiss; if the bottle was shaken first, the gas rushes out all at once and sprays everywhere.
fact: A pressure cooker traps steam so the pressure rises and water boils at a higher temperature, which cooks food faster; it has a safety valve and only grown-ups use it.
fact: On a high mountain the air presses less, so water boils below 100 degrees and pasta and eggs take longer to cook.
fact: A balloon shrinks in the freezer and grows again when warmed, because cold air pushes less on the rubber.
fact: A jar closed while hot has a lid held down by the outside air once it cools, and the lid pops as air rushes in when you open it.
fact: If the safety button on a new jar lid is already up in the shop, the seal may be broken and the food may be bad.
exercise: How does a vacuum cleaner really pick up dust? | Its fan lowers the pressure inside and outside air pushes in | It pulls the dust with a magnet | It makes the dust lighter than air | Its nozzle sticks to the dust like glue
exercise: Why does a bicycle tyre go flat after a puncture? | The squashed air inside escapes to the lower pressure outside | The rubber shrinks tightly around the hole and squeezes all the air away | The air inside turns into water | The air is pulled out by the road
exercise: How can you find a tiny hole in a bike's inner tube? | Put it in soapy water and look for bubbles | Hold it up to a bright light and look for a gleam | Weigh it on the kitchen scales before and after | Leave it in the freezer overnight and look for frost
exercise: Why does ketchup shoot out when you squeeze the bottle? | The squeeze raises the pressure all through the bottle | The ketchup gets hotter in your hand and so it runs much faster | The bottle cap pulls the sauce out | Squeezing makes the ketchup lighter
exercise: Why does water go into a syringe when you pull the plunger? | The pressure inside drops and the outside air pushes water in | The plunger pulls the water up with a thin string hidden inside the tube | The water climbs the walls by itself | The syringe is magnetic for water
exercise: What makes air go into your lungs when you breathe in? | Your chest gets bigger, pressure drops, and outside air pushes in | Your nose pulls the air in like a magnet | Your lungs blow air out to make room | Your heart pumps air into your lungs
exercise: Why does a fizzy drink bottle hiss when you open it? | Gas squashed into it under pressure escapes | Air rushes into the bottle from the room outside | The drink starts to boil as soon as you open it | The plastic snaps back into shape with a loud click
exercise: Why does a pressure cooker cook food faster? | The higher pressure lets water get hotter before it boils | The steam gets colder under pressure and so cooks the food gently | It squeezes the food into smaller pieces | The lid keeps the light in
exercise: Why does a pressure cooker have a safety valve? | Trapped steam can be dangerous if the pressure gets too high | To let the lovely smell of the cooking food out into the kitchen | To let you peek at the food | To keep the steam cold
exercise: At what temperature does water boil on a very high mountain? | Below 100 degrees | Exactly 100 degrees | Above 100 degrees | It cannot boil up there at all
exercise: Why do eggs take longer to cook high on a mountain? | The water boils at a lower temperature, so it is not as hot | Mountain eggs have thicker shells | The cold wind blows the heat off the pan | Water cannot boil at all up there
exercise: What happens to a balloon put in the freezer? | It shrinks, then grows back when it warms | It bursts straight away with a loud bang in the cold | It grows bigger and bigger as it gets colder | It freezes solid and turns into a ball of ice
exercise: Why does a new jar lid pop when you open it? | Air rushes in where the cooled air inside pressed less | The jam inside pushes the lid up | The lid was glued down at the factory and the glue breaks with a pop | The glass shrinks when you twist it
exercise: The safety button on a new jar lid is already up in the shop. What should you think? | The seal may be broken, so the food may be bad | The jam is extra fresh | The jar was filled with too much jam at the factory | That is how all good jars look
exercise: Which rule do tyres, fizzy drinks and vacuum cleaners all show? | Air moves from higher pressure to lower pressure | Air always moves upwards, away from the ground below | Air moves from cold places to warm places, never back | Air moves only when it is pushed along by some kind of fan
test: oi, why's me football all soft now? pumped it up in the house, now it's frosty out | Cold air inside pushes less, so the ball feels softer | Footballs always leak in winter | The frost sucks the air out of it through the leather overnight | The ball shrank because the leather froze
test: Your friend says a hoover "sucks the dirt in". What would a physicist say? | Outside air pushes the dirt in where the pressure is lower | Hoovers really pull the dirt with suction rays | The dirt jumps in because it likes the dark | The dirt is sucked by static only
test: A camper on a very high mountain says his pasta is still hard after the usual time. What would help? | Cook it longer, or use a pressure cooker | Turn the water down so it stops bubbling | Put in more salt to make it boil colder | Take the lid off to trap more heat
test: Crap, mum shook the cola bottle before handing it to me. What happens if I open it now? | The gas rushes out at once and sprays everywhere | Nothing, shaking has no effect | The cola goes flat inside the closed bottle straight away | The bottle will freeze in your hand
test: What would happen if you tried to breathe in with your chest unable to get any bigger? | No air would come in, because the pressure inside could not drop | Air would rush in even faster | You would breathe through your ears instead | Your heart would do the breathing for you
test: A ketchup bottle gurgles when you stop squeezing. What is happening? | It springs back and air is pushed in to fill the space | The ketchup is boiling inside the bottle from the warmth of your hand | The ketchup is slowly turning into air and bubbling away inside | The bottle is cracking and breaking at the bottom where you squeezed
test: Which shows warmth making air push harder? | A cold balloon growing back to full size as it warms | A tyre going flat from a nail | Ketchup squirting out of a bottle when you squeeze it hard | A sucker sticking to the tiles
test: In a cartoon a cook opens a pressure cooker the moment it stops hissing and smiles. What is the real rule? | Only a grown-up opens it, after the pressure is safely let out | Anyone can open it whenever they like | Pressure cookers are never hot inside | You open it fastest by pulling hard on the lid
test: How is breathing in like pulling back the plunger of a syringe? | Both work by making more room so the outside air pushes in | Both blow air out first so that they can pull more air back in | Both use a fan to move air | Neither depends on air pressure at all
test: You leave a hot jar of homemade jam to cool with the lid on tight. A day later the lid is pulled in. Why? | The cooled air inside presses less than the air outside | The jam shrank and pulled the lid down by sticking to it | The lid got cold and shrank more than the glass | Someone squeezed it in the night
test: Would a vacuum cleaner work on the Moon, with no air at all? | No, there is no outside air to push the dust in | Yes, and it would work even better there than at home | Yes, but it would only pick up the grey Moon dust | Only if you turned it right up to full power first
exam: Identify the principal reason for which water boils at a reduced temperature at high altitude. | The atmospheric pressure acting upon the water is diminished | The water is of a different chemical composition | The flame burns more fiercely in the thin air found at high altitude | The vessel conducts heat less readily
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of a vacuum cleaner? | The external air, at higher pressure, propels the dust into a region of reduced pressure | The dust is attracted by a magnetic field within the device | The dust is rendered lighter than the surrounding air | The nozzle adheres to the dust chemically
exam: It is given that the volume of the chest cavity is enlarged during inhalation. Determine the immediate consequence. | The pressure within the lungs diminishes and external air enters | The pressure within the lungs increases and air is expelled | The heart propels air into the lungs | No movement of air occurs
exam: State the reason for which a pressure cooker is fitted with a safety valve. | Excessive pressure of confined steam would constitute a hazard | The valve serves solely to release the odours of the food being cooked | The valve permits the food to be observed | The valve cools the steam to below boiling
